=== Levels 48–99: Rubium rocks ===
Mining [[rubium rocks]] in the [[Charred Dungeon]] offers a low-effort and profitable, but slower alternative for players wishing to avoid tick manipulation. Rocks are solely located in the dungeon on [[Charred Island]], requiring level 60 [[Sailing]] and a one-time use of a [[rope]] (found on the island) to access. The island can be easily reached from [[Red Rock]] (requiring partial completion of the [[Red Reef]] quest), which can be teleported to via the [[Sailors' amulet]] and has a [[Shipwright Sylvia|Shipwright]] to negate the trip back, or alternatively from the [[Void Knights' Outpost]]. As the mining spots are past aggressive [[Red dragon|red]] and [[Black dragon|black dragons]], dragonfire protection (''e.g.'' a [[Antifire potion|potion]] or [[Anti-dragon shield|shield]]) or level 37 [[Prayer]] for [[Protect from Magic]] is highly recommended.

Players can expect ~39,000 XP/h at level 48, scaling up to ~64,000 XP at level 99. [[Mining enhancers]], including the [[Varrock armour]], the [[prospector kit]] and [[Celestial ring|celestial rings]]/[[Celestial signet|signets]] can all improve rates. While there is a buildable [[Bank chest (amenity)|bank chest]] on the island (requiring level 70 [[Construction]]), using it is not necessary for optimal rates, as [[rubium splinters]] stack in the inventory.

===Levels 60–99: Crashed stars===
Mining [[crashed star]]s is a very low-effort alternative to the faster methods. As part of the [[Shooting Stars]] [[Distraction and Diversion]], a shooting star will fall at a random spot selected from a predetermined list. Once the player has found a crashed star, they can mine it for 42–63 minutes depending on its tier before the star depletes. Each tier can be mined for seven minutes without interaction, only requiring the player to click again whenever the star degrades a tier.

There are a few issues this method faces if one chooses to efficiently train on crashed stars. Depending on the size of the star, players need to have a secondary activity planned after fully mining stars to depletion. Using an [[oak telescope]] or better in a [[Study|study room]] of a [[player-owned house]] can display a timeframe for when the star will fall, as well as the region it will land in. Stars may also fall in the [[Wilderness]] and in other dangerous areas, which are not recommended to train on. Switching worlds or receiving information from other players/clans may also help to scout for new crashed stars.

This method is fully available at level 60 Mining since the lowest star falls at tier 6, but it can begin at level 10 if the star is mined down to a suitable tier. The [[stardust]] can be exchanged for a [[celestial ring]], which gives an invisible +4 boost to Mining. Each piece of stardust is valued at {{Coins|{{GEP|Soft clay|100}}/150}} when buying a [[soft clay pack]], or at {{Coins|{{Average drop value|Bag full of gems|raw=y}} / 300}} when buying a [[bag full of gems]] from [[Dusuri's Star Shop]].

If stardust is used to charge the celestial ring and is taken to efficient [[Pay-to-play Mining training#Levels 45–99: Granite|Granite]] mining, each hour of mining stardust is worth ~10k xp/h more and can give 140k xp/h with good clicks.

Experience and stardust rates are shown in the table below. The rates include the time spent finding a new star as well as some idle time whenever the star degrades a tier. While it's possible to use tick manipulation for higher rates, doing so is not recommended for this method (except for obtaining stardust as quickly as possible) as there are much better options for high-effort training.

